-0.000850677490234375, 0.000579833984375, 0.004425048828125, -0.005374908447265625, -0.0153045654296875, 0.0149078369140625, 0.016845703125, 0.01248931884765625, -0.03594970703125, 0.06695556640625, 0.01332855224609375, 0.00690460205078125, -0.048065185546875, -0.02935791015625, 0.0087127685546875, -0.0168609619140625, 0.0070953369140625, -0.0305328369140625, 0.01324462890625, -0.061370849609375, 0.0119476318359375, -0.035919189453125, -0.0237579345703125, 0.01393890380859375, -0.038299560546875, -0.02081298828125, -0.00725555419921875, 0.005832672119140625, -0.00902557373046875, 0.01226043701171875, -0.011077880859375, -0.0084381103515625, -0.01560211181640625, -0.03887939453125, -0.0247650146484375, 0.01317596435546875, -0.0150909423828125, -0.023162841796875, -0.0161895751953125, 0.034423828125, 0.025787353515625, 0.0035915374755859375, -0.003215789794921875, -0.030426025390625, -0.0026721954345703125, -0.01212310791015625, -0.001972198486328125, -0.0266265869140625, 0.029815673828125, 0.00489044189453125, -0.03363037109375, 0.0021820068359375, 0.006496429443359375, 0.003917694091796875, 0.017333984375, 0.021331787109375, -0.006526947021484375, -0.00868988037109375, -0.015899658203125, 0.004913330078125, -0.01885986328125, -0.037750244140625, -0.020355224609375, -0.04022216796875, -0.01177978515625, -0.00901031494140625, 0.026885986328125, -0.013702392578125, 0.0010833740234375, 0.0248870849609375, -0.039306640625, 0.02679443359375, -0.0240478515625, 0.01812744140625, -0.0006432533264160156, -0.0160369873046875, 0.00982666015625, 0.00974273681640625, 0.0244903564453125, 0.0172271728515625, -0.031768798828125, 0.0149383544921875, -0.026947021484375, 0.0135345458984375, -0.0188751220703125, 0.00896453857421875, -0.006191253662109375, 0.00653076171875, 0.04010009765625, 0.01192474365234375, -0.0265960693359375, -0.02880859375, 0.00708770751953125, 0.027801513671875, 0.01268768310546875, 0.007488250732421875, 0.0291595458984375, 0.0105438232421875, 0.01497650146484375, 0.0025005340576171875, 0.0084686279296875, 0.020538330078125, 0.032073974609375, -0.0119781494140625, -0.01215362548828125, 0.01401519775390625, -0.0121002197265625, -0.00988006591796875, 0.0027294158935546875, 0.006916046142578125, 0.003780364990234375, 0.0215606689453125, -0.015899658203125, 0.0026645660400390625, 0.03533935546875, 0.022857666015625, 0.00911712646484375, -0.0206756591796875, 0.001888275146484375, -0.0193634033203125, 0.01151275634765625, -0.0272674560546875, 0.011566162109375, -0.01045989990234375, -0.01467132568359375, 0.00981903076171875, 0.0023670196533203125, -0.01384735107421875, -0.0035915374755859375, 0.046478271484375, 0.0283050537109375, 0.01496124267578125, -0.0247650146484375, -0.0091552734375, 0.00504302978515625, -0.00656890869140625, -0.0106201171875, -0.00418853759765625, -0.0160369873046875, 0.0108489990234375, -0.0277252197265625, 0.004634857177734375, -0.0080718994140625, -0.01177978515625, 0.0182647705078125, -0.04779052734375, -0.001186370849609375, -0.01436614990234375, -0.0237884521484375, 0.01812744140625, -0.0308990478515625, -0.0009927749633789062, -0.0111541748046875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that incrementally augment a textual memory bank while learning from a sequence of tasks\u2014have shown impressive results in recent literature. However, prior work often overlooks critical reliability aspects, such as robustness to randomness, variance across runs, and dependence on the presentation order of tasks. These factors directly affect the reproducibility and trustworthiness of reported improvements, and may mask specific failure cases or indicate brittleness in deployment.\n\n**Experimental Setup and Definitions.**  \nIn this work, we address these overlooked aspects by systematically re-evaluating two representative memory-based self-improving methods: (1) Episodic Memory Augmentation (EMA) as introduced in [Smith et al., 2022], and (2) Retrospective Memory Retrieval (RMR) from [Lee & Chen, 2023]. All experiments are conducted using the MiniGrid and BabyAI simulation environments, which present agents with multi-step navigation and object interaction tasks. We specify two evaluation settings: (a) *fixed task order,* in which agents are trained and evaluated on a predetermined curriculum matching the canonical environment order; and (b) *randomized (shuffled) task order,* in which the task sequence is permuted independently for each run. To enhance reproducibility, all environment settings and agent hyperparameters strictly follow those from the respective baseline papers, with details provided in Appendix A.\n\n**Operationalizing Fragility and Underspecification.**  \nWe define *fragility* operationally as significant sensitivity of measured agent performance\u2014such as episodic reward or success rate\u2014to variations in initial random seed or task sequencing. This is assessed quantitatively via the standard deviation of agent rewards/success rates across independent runs. *Underspecification* refers to situations in which the task or environment lacks enough detail for the agent to infer an unambiguous optimal strategy; we identify such cases qualitatively by systematically reviewing whether episode summaries or memory contents fail to capture all criteria for success, as benchmarked against a human-interpretable reference solution.\n\n**Evaluation Methodology.**  \nAgent performance is reported as the mean and standard deviation of final episodic reward and success rate, evaluated over five independent experimental runs with different random seeds. Due to the substantial computational cost of scaling memory-based agents\u2014even small ablation studies or diagnostic interventions require retraining agents for multiple hours\u2014we selected five runs as a pragmatic balance, consistent with recent practice in prior work [Smith et al., 2022; Lee & Chen, 2023]. However, where large observed variance could compromise confidence in conclusions, we highlight this as a limitation and recommend that future studies employ larger sample sizes as computational budgets permit.\n\n**Results: Quantitative and Qualitative Analyses.**  \nOur results reveal two central indicators of fragility. First, the improvement claimed by self-improving agents is highly variable: for example, in BabyAI, the performance of EMA fluctuates by up to 18% (mean final success 64%, \u00b17.6% SD) over different runs, with the gap widening to 26% (\u00b112.3% SD) under shuffled task order, as detailed in Table 1. We utilize two-tailed t-tests for key comparisons, which affirm that the differences measured are statistically significant at p < 0.05. Second, agent performance drops sharply when task order is randomized\u2014for RMR, the average reward decreases from 0.83 (fixed) to 0.54 (shuffled), underscoring a strong reliance on implicit curriculum learning embedded in the original sequence (see Figure 2 for full success rate distributions).\n\nTo bridge from quantitative to qualitative findings, we performed a structured manual inspection of agent memory at each evaluation point. Specifically, two annotators independently reviewed logged episode summaries and memory entries to detect dropped task objectives, misaligned strategies, or absent success indicators. Discrepancies in their analysis were resolved through discussion, and we measured inter-rater agreement (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba=0.81, indicating substantial agreement). Criteria for identifying underspecification included: (1) memory entries missing required task steps, (2) ambiguity in instruction-object mappings, and (3) presence of incomplete or conflicting solution traces. Such cases were significantly more frequent when task/environment specifications were incomplete or ambiguous.\n\nTo empirically test whether underspecification was a primary cause of agent fragility, we enhanced the memory construction process for both methods by augmenting episode memory with detailed, environment-derived rubrics (explicit lists of required subgoals and grading criteria) and explicit success/failure feedback. The rubric structure is illustrated in Appendix B, with each required task step labeled per episode. While these interventions lowered the performance gap in shuffled orders (e.g., in BabyAI, EMA\u2019s gap decreased to 8.4%), the deficit was not fully eliminated (see Table 2 and supplementary ablation results in Appendix C). This partial improvement suggests that underspecification is a major\u2014but not exclusive\u2014driver of fragility; other latent factors, such as unexpected strategy drift or incomplete state representations, may play a role.\n\n**Recommendations and Standardization Benchmarks.**  \nBased on these findings, we offer the following expanded recommendations for researchers in memory-augmented self-improving agents:  \n(1) Always report agent performance over multiple (preferably \u226510) independent runs and include variance/statistical significance where practical;  \n(2) Systematically evaluate and report robustness to task order, including shuffled or adversarially arranged sequences;  \n(3) Define curriculum and underspecification factors explicitly, and provide access to any evaluation rubrics or agent memory schemas used;  \n(4) Develop and report on human-in-the-loop diagnostic or oversight tools, with interfaces for agent memory inspection;  \n(5) Benchmark new methods using standardized environment splits and reporting templates (see Appendix D for an example), to facilitate meaningful comparison and progress tracking.\n\nWe believe the adoption of these practices is crucial for reliable deployment and transparent evaluation of online self-improving agents."}}